    "q-65278": {
      "dom_id": "step-q65278",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "What is the maximum value of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+2?$",
      "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, shift it by $2$ units **up.**\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254493/assets/a6b16fb37068de09fec669e0368454069d1e1853066dd4e7aaffee6be8255e23.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the maximum value is $y=3.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe know that $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ has a maximum value of $1.$ \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le 1\n$$\n\nAdding $2$ to both sides of the above inequality gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2 & \\le 1+2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2 & \\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2$ is $3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
